const title = 'Does a Trust Have to Be Notarized? | FreeTrustDocs';
const description = 'Does a trust have to be notarized? Most states accept a signature, but notarized deeds and certificates of trust are often required. State rules inside.';
---

<Base title={title} description={description} ogType="article">
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="breadcrumbs">
        <a href="/">Home</a> <span>›</span> <a href="/guide/">Guides</a> <span>›</span> Notarization
      </div>
      <h1>Does a Trust Have to Be Notarized?</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Short answer: usually no — but several related documents almost always do. Most states require only that
        the trust instrument be signed by the settlor. The exceptions are Louisiana (witnesses required) and a
        handful of states where state-specific rules apply.
      </p>

      <StatBand
        stats={[
          { figure: 'No*', label: 'The trust itself, most states', note: 'The settlor\u2019s signature is usually enough' },
          { figure: 'Yes', label: 'Deed to real property', note: 'Notarized before the county records it' },
          { figure: 'Often', label: 'Certificate for banks', note: 'Wanted in affidavit form' },
        ]}
      />

      <h2>The General Rule</h2>
      <p>
        Under the Uniform Trust Code, which most states have adopted in some form, a trust is created when the
        settlor has capacity, a valid trust purpose exists, and the trust instrument is executed with the
        formalities state law requires. For a standard revocable living trust, that formality is usually just the
        settlor's signature — a notary's seal is not what makes the trust valid.
      </p>

      <h2>Where You Do Need a Notary</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>The deed transferring real property into the trust.</strong> Virtually every state requires the
          deed to be notarized (acknowledged) before it can be recorded with the county. This is the step people
          most often mistake as a "trust notarization" — it's the deed, not the trust itself.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Certificates of trust for banks and title companies.</strong> Many institutions want the
          certificate in affidavit form with a notarized signature before they'll rely on it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>County-specific practice.</strong> Some recorders reject unnotarized supporting documents even
          when the statute doesn't require it.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <h2>State Exceptions</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Louisiana</strong> — trusts must be in writing with the signature witnessed by two witnesses
          and notarized (La. R.S. 9:1732). This is the clearest notarization requirement of any state.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Ohio</strong> — trusts holding Ohio real estate are effective only if the trust instrument is
          recorded with the county recorder (R.C. 5801.401). Recording brings its own notarization requirements
          in practice.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Tennessee</strong> — revocable trusts can be created orally if the settlor expresses an intent
          to create one and a trustee holds property, but written trusts follow the general signature rule.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Michigan</strong> — no notary required for validity, but certificates of trust must be in the
          form of an affidavit (MCL § 700.7913), which is typically notarized.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What This Means for Your Documents</h2>
      <p>
        A trust instrument without a notarization block is still valid in most states — but plan for the
        notary anyway. If the trust holds real estate, the deed must be notarized to record. And banks routinely
        ask for a notarized certificate of trust before opening accounts or wiring proceeds.
      </p>

      <h2>Notarizing Documents Generated Here</h2>
      <p>
        FreeTrustDocs generates a Certificate of Trust, a Declaration of Trust, and a Land Trust Agreement. Take
        the printed documents to any notary public — banks, UPS stores, and county clerk offices all offer
        notary services, many at no charge for account holders. The wizard pages list your state's execution
        requirements before you finalize.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="/create/certificate-of-trust/">Create a Certificate of Trust →</a>
      </p>

        <section class="section-tight">
    <div class="container-narrow">
      <h2>Related guides</h2>
      <ul class="sources-list">
        <li><a href="/guide/what-is-a-certificate-of-trust/">What is a Certificate of Trust?</a></li>
        <li><a href="/guide/certificate-of-trust-vs-declaration/">Certificate of Trust vs Declaration of Trust</a></li>
        <li><a href="/certificate-of-trust/all-states/">Certificate of Trust — All 50 States Guide</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section-tight">
    <div class="container-narrow">
      <h2>When a notary is required</h2>
      <p>The general rule is simple, but the exceptions are where mistakes happen. This decision path mirrors the sections below — most trust documents themselves do not legally require notarization, while anything recorded (notably deeds into a land trust) always does.</p>
      <figure class="network-figure guide-figure" aria-label="Decision flow for whether a trust document needs a notary">
        <svg class="network-svg" viewBox="0 0 720 300" role="img" aria-labelledby="notary-svg-title notary-svg-desc" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
          <title id="notary-svg-title">Notarization decision flow for trust documents</title>
          <desc id="notary-svg-desc">A trust declaration or certificate generally does not require a notary; a recorded deed into a land trust always requires notarization; many banks expect notarization regardless.</desc>
          <g class="network-lines" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1">
            <path d="M92 150 C170 150 190 150 262 150" />
            <path d="M370 150 C440 150 450 84 514 84" />
            <path d="M370 150 C440 150 450 150 514 150" />
            <path d="M370 150 C440 150 450 216 514 216" />
          </g>
          <g class="network-nodes">
            <g class="network-node network-node--input"><circle cx="76" cy="150" r="9"/><text x="76" y="123" text-anchor="middle">YOUR TRUST</text></g>
            <g class="network-node network-node--core"><circle cx="316" cy="150" r="20"/><circle cx="316" cy="150" r="27" class="network-ring"/><text x="316" y="155" text-anchor="middle">DOC TYPE</text></g>
            <g class="network-node network-node--structure"><circle cx="530" cy="84" r="9"/><text x="530" y="57" text-anchor="middle">RECORDED DEED — NOTARY REQUIRED</text></g>
            <g class="network-node network-node--input"><circle cx="530" cy="150" r="9"/><text x="530" y="177" text-anchor="middle">DECLARATION / CERT — NO LEGAL REQUIREMENT</text></g>
            <g class="network-node network-node--structure"><circle cx="530" cy="216" r="9"/><text x="530" y="243" text-anchor="middle">BANK POLICY — OFTEN EXPECTS IT</text></g>
          </g>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>Fig. 1 — Notarization is a property of what you are doing with the document, not of the document itself.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>
<div class="upl-notice" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
